\title{RLVM agentic journal}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Working notes for RLVM (Rabdos Lean VM), a checker for the Lean 4 kernel on GraalVM. Agents and the maintainer record design notes, decisions, measured findings, open problems, examples and dated changelog entries here as small trees that link to each other. A tree states one point; the long research, design and review documents stay in the repository under \code{docs/} and are linked from the trees. The journal is documentation under the GNU Free Documentation License; see the [licence](licence).}

\subtree[index-what]{
\title{What RLVM is}

\p{RLVM reads an export of a Lean 4 library in the format of \code{lean4export} and answers accept, reject or decline, as an external checker for the Lean Kernel Arena does. It is written in Java for GraalVM and runs as a native image or on the JVM.}

\p{The maintainer's framing is that the project is a \em{mechanisation} of the kernel's type checking in the sense of type theory: the judgments of the kernel (inference, checking, conversion, reduction to weak-head normal form) are entries of a worklist with continuations, and RLVM is the machine that steps that worklist. Its design is written as an instruction set for normalisation by evaluation and definitional equality. The goal is real checking performance, which means warm wall-clock time with parallel workers, with the verdicts of the official checker. Both are stated in the [standing policies](standing-policies).}

\p{There are two engines behind one pipeline of loader, store, admission and driver. The \em{reference engine} is a port of the official algorithm over a hash-consed term store: it admits inductive types, re-checks every rejection, is the oracle of the differential tests and is the strict mode. The \em{machine} is the worklist engine on values that is meant to carry the checking work.}
}

\subtree[index-state]{
\title{Where it stands}

\p{State of 2026-11-24, the second day of the project, on branch \code{dev}.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Verdicts.} The pipeline accepts all eight corpora it is measured on (Init, Std, cedar, cslib, con-leche, reachability, navier-stokes-euler and Mathlib) and gives the expected verdict on the 230 published arena tests without a decline. On 17,775 mutated exports with stored official verdicts it has no wrong accept and no wrong reject.}
\li{\strong{Default engine.} The reference engine is the default; the machine is selected with \code{\verb~|-X engine=machine~}. The machine was the default engine for under an hour on 2026-11-24 and was taken back when new hostile exports showed accepts that the official checker rejects. Those were fixed or classified, the maintainer chose practical exactness, and an adversarial review of the candidate default configuration found no accept of an underivable statement. One launcher option selects the policy, practical or strict. What the default may differ in is listed under [accepted risks](accepted-risks); what is left before the machine is the default again is in [the problem tree](prob-W2FV) and [the decision](dec-T79V).}
\li{\strong{Speed.} Warm wall clock with 8 workers and pipelined loading, on the aarch64 development host, in the tables of \src{docs/BENCHMARKS.md} as generated on the evening of 2026-11-24. On all of Mathlib the machine with its default switch set takes 21.2 s on the JVM launcher and 28.2 s as a native image; measured in the same windows on the same cores, the two leading checkers of the arena take 14.2 s (gruszka) and 18.7 s (nano-lean), and the default configuration with the reference engine takes 58.0 s on the JVM launcher. On Init, Std and cedar the machine on the JVM launcher takes 0.62 s, 1.22 s and 1.61 s, which is 0.87 to 0.95 times gruszka's time. The session trees quote the tables of earlier in the day, which were taken with other distributions in other windows.}
\li{\strong{Where the gap is.} Profiles of Mathlib attribute most of the remaining distance to the official rule for comparing proofs, which the leading checkers do not follow, then to declarations that enter eager reduction, then to a uniform cost per operation; see [the problem tree](prob-TJK3). Both of the first two have a switch that is off by default. The first tables of an itemised cost profile, which are working tables and not yet a document, have one warm pass over Mathlib on the JVM launcher at 21.9 s with the default switch set, 18.1 s with eager reduction on the machine, 15.7 s with the two proof-comparison shortcuts and 13.1 s with both. Whether they are on by default is decided by such measurements over all corpora.}
}
}

\subtree[index-hubs]{
\title{Contents}

\ul{
\li{[Design notes](designs): how each part is meant to work and why, in a reading order.}
\li{[Decisions](decisions): what was chosen, against which alternatives, and what follows from it.}
\li{[Insights](insights): measured findings, with their numbers and conditions, and hypotheses not yet measured.}
\li{[Reports](reports): the full record of each measurement campaign, review and spike.}
\li{[Studies](studies): readings of outside sources - the arena, the official kernel, other checkers, techniques.}
\li{[Open problems](open-problems): what is not settled, what is known, and what would close it.}
\li{[Examples](examples): inputs that show a rule of the kernel or a wrong verdict.}
\li{[Changelog](changelog): dated entries, by month and [by area](release-unreleased).}
\li{[Sessions](sessions): one tree per phase of the work, with what was done and where it is recorded.}
\li{[Standing policies](standing-policies): the maintainer's lasting instructions, with their dates.}
\li{[The owner's notes](owner-notes): the maintainer's instructions 1 to 16 as they were given, each with its reading.}
\li{[Accepted risks](accepted-risks): trade-offs decided on purpose, among them where the default configuration may differ from the official checker.}
\li{[People and agents](agents): who wrote what.}
\li{[Licence](licence).}
}
}
